.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1053 PM PDT Sat Aug 29 2026
(Tonight through Monday)

Saturday was a beautiful sunny day across the Bay Area and Central
Coast and we`ll get more of the same on Sunday. The marine layer
stratus fully evaporated by noon as a much direr air mass moved in
from the north. The 00Z weather balloon measured the precipitable
water at 0.58", which is around the 10th percentile for this time of
year. With unimpeded radiative cooling, marine layer stratus is
starting to reform, with Half Moon Bay recording the first clouds
overhead this evening. Coastal areas south of the Golden Gate
will likely see the stratus come back before the sun rises Sunday
morning. Inland valleys are more of a toss-up. The marine layer is
currently 1,600` deep and will gradually expand over the next 24
hours. Any clouds that form will likely clear by midday with a
mild, breezy, and clear afternoon.

Marine layer stratus should be much better organized by Monday
morning. The depth will likely be around 2,000` by then, with less
chance of clearing along the coast. As a short wave trough moves
through, temperatures will also be around 5 degrees cooler than the
weekend with a stronger onshore breeze in the afternoon.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1053 PM PDT Sat Aug 29 2026
(Monday night through next Saturday)

As the troughing pattern continues, the 850 mb temperature will
bottom out around 6-10 C on Tuesday. That`s about as low as it gets
this time of year and more like a normal November day. High
temperatures will be in the 60s and 70s with the typical marine
layer stratus in the morning. The troughing pattern will deepen and
a cut-off low will bring some early rain to the Pacific Northwest
later this week. There is a chance for some light rain to make it as
far south as the North Bay starting Tuesday. The ECMWF ensemble mean
shows a few hundredths at Santa Rosa from Tuesday through Friday,
with a 10% chance of reaching 1/2". The probability drops quickly
further south outside of some marine layer drizzle along the
coast. This cut-off low now looks like it will linger near
Northern California into the weekend. If so, this low pressure
breaks up the normal subtropical high and opens the door for
tropical cyclones and the associated moisture to recurve to the
north rather than being held in the tropical latitudes. While it`s
too early to say if that will happen, the persistent troughing
pattern looks like it may keep the door open into next week.

